"q-325154/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ceca959-9b75-53ed-b311-565fc531af71" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325154/field-2/974f1e09406a3c6be062f79ae2c99a3f9162780580a069e1cc5bf211a15adf21" :answer-field/choices ["q-325154/field-2/974f1e09406a3c6be062f79ae2c99a3f9162780580a069e1cc5bf211a15adf21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325154" :question/id #uuid "b54a34b9-1948-5f8e-8260-327185f8c907" :question/math-academy-id "q-325154" :question/problem "Find the **Cartesian coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ intersects the positive $y$-axis.\n\nThe point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{5}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{13}{5}),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/86158ced718c422c9b02d620ca4544eb7d2ff0e110a04fe4b7f749160fc46649.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $(0,\\frac{13}{5}\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325154/field-1" "q-325154/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f33baf-c3b5-549a-8eb7-1ab370e9c62b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325154"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325173/field-1/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" :answer/id #uuid "de316200-1dd3-5b35-a155-836c25d932b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325173/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "eeeeff73-0133-5f30-bddb-1b150ed00d27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325173/field-1/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" :answer-field/choices ["q-325173/field-1/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325173" :question/id #uuid "fecdf4e9-12e3-5d4e-8c67-f9e63dd592f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-325173" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/fecfcefdf82c8ae08dd8788d2a80df0a717c04344042bb38371c1dd5df95f670.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(2,-1)$, as shown above, is$\\,\\,r=$ \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325173/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325173"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
